  "text": "㬊At first, we need to identify the possible values for base $b$. We know that the digits are alphabetical and starting from 'A' are used, and from the equation, we see that $b > N_D$ and $b > N_I$. This means that base $b$ must be greater than the second digit in the alphabetical order, which is 'F' (7). Also, $b$ must be an integer greater than 1. So, the possible values for $b$ are: 8, 9, and 10. However, since $b > 7$ and $b < 75$, we can eliminate the possibility of $b$ being 8 or 9.\n\nOur remaining option is to check if base 10 works with the equation. To do this, we need to correctly interpret the equation in base 10, taking the base 10 range into account:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} && A&B&C&D_{10} \\\\ & + & B&C&D&A_{10} \\\\ \\cline{2-6} & E&F&G&H&I_{10} \\\\ \\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe will now analyze the equation row by row by considering the possible values for each digit.\n\nIn the hundreds place (0th place), $A$ and $B$ have the same value because $N_A = N_B$ in any position, which gives us:\n\n$$A = B$$\n\n\nIn the tens place (1st place), we have $C$ in the left column, $D$ in the right column, $E$ in the middle column, and $N_C$ and $N_D$ for the alphabetic digits. So, we would have:\n\n$$E = C + D + 10$$\n\nwhere $10$ is carried over because the sum of $C$ and $D$ in the right column is greater than or equal to 11.\n\nThe hundredths digit places (2nd place) simply adds $F$ and $N_I$.\n\nNow, finding the highest values that are consistent for the expanded equation looking towards the thousands place, which means columns 2 and 3 provide multiples of $1000$:\n\n$$\\begin{align}\nI &= F + G + 1000 \\\\\nG &= N_G - 1000 \\\\\n& N_G - G = I - F \\tag{1} \\\\\n\\end{align}$$\n\nAfterwards, solving equation (1), we balance our alphabetic positions pulling the correct numbers based on the consistent carries within the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} && A&B&C&D_{10} \\\\ & + & B&C&D&A_{10} \\\\ \\cline{2-6} & E&